            "lens": "review-resilience",
            "findings": [
              {
                "id": "R4-001",
                "lens": "resilience",
                "location": "engine/synctrigger/synctrigger.go:355-356",
                "severity": "WARNING",
                "claim": "Rotation is check-then-rename without locking. Two concurrent triggers can both see a full log and each rotate, so the second shifts the first's fresh .1 file up and can drop an older log. Older diagnostics are lost, but no line being written is lost. The code comment and task note already document this, so it is a bounded and accepted degradation.",
                "proof_refs": [
                  "engine/synctrigger/synctrigger.go:355-356"
                ]
              }
            ],
            "evidence": [
              "Reviewed all 22 manifest paths for failure handling, retry safety, latency and load.",
              "The stderr copy kept for classification is capped at 64 KiB by boundedBuffer, which still reports full writes, so the MultiWriter keeps sending everything to the log. Memory use of the child is bounded as intended.",
              "The log is bounded at 1 MiB with three rotated files. Rotation and chmod are best effort and never block the sync, so a failure in either degrades gracefully.",
              "A non-absolute state directory is refused in openLog and in RunChild with error:log and a skipped binary. The caller still exits 0, which preserves the always-exit-0 contract, and the hook never fails the session. The golden for the no-HOME case confirms this.",
              "logField quoting prevents log-line forgery without changing the format of plain paths."
            ]
          }
        }
      },
